stream
<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?><x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="xmp-writer"><rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"><rdf:Description rdf:about="" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"  xmlns:xmp="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/"  xmlns:xmpMM="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/mm/"  xmlns:xmpTPg="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/t/pg/"  xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/" ><dc:title><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Playtimeâ€“wellbeing associations are practically null across video game genres: a multi-platform digital trace registered report</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:title><dc:description><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Video game research often treats games as a monolithic category, and single-game or single-platform studies leave open whether findings generalise across the medium. In this registered report, we used multi-platform digital trace data from the Open Play dataset to test whether (H1) total playtime is meaningfully associated with mental wellbeing and (H2) whether this association differs by game genre. The data comprise 226,000 hours of playtime telemetry across 4,154 titles from Nintendo, Xbox, and Steam for 1,838 UK and US adults, linked to structured genre metadata and to 6,680 biweekly mental wellbeing assessments (SWEMWBS). Bayesian multilevel within-between models evaluated both hypotheses against a pre-registered threshold for practical significance. Total playtime was practically equivalent to zero on both dimensions: wellbeing barely moved in waves when participants played more than their own average, and those who habitually played more reported slightly lower wellbeing, well under the threshold of practical significance. Contrary to our registered prediction that genres would differ in how playtime relates to fluctuations in mental wellbeing, genre differences fell below the threshold across three model specifications, including a multi-membership model for games carrying multiple genre labels. Genres proved statistically distinguishable but practically interchangeable. A further exploratory analysis extended these tests to the daily timescale and found the same result, namely a small positive within-person trend far below practical significance and a between-person contrast leaning negative within the equivalence region. The playtimeâ€“wellbeing null thus survives its strictest test to date and generalises across genres, with slight heterogeneity detected and bounded below any practically meaningful magnitude. Genre differences appear to be the wrong place to look for gaming&apos;s wellbeing effects; genre labels are too coarse a proxy for the psychological experiences that plausibly drive them, which directs attention towards finer-grained characterisations of play.</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:description><pdf:Keywords>video games, wellbeing, genres, digital trace data, registered report</pdf:Keywords><dc:creator><rdf:Seq><rdf:li>Thomas Hakman, Matti Vuorre, Nick Ballou, TamÃ¡s Andrei FÃ¶ldes, Kristoffer Magnusson, Andrew K Przybylski</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:creator><xmp:CreatorTool>Typst 0.14.2</xmp:CreatorTool><dc:language><rdf:Bag><rdf:li>en-US</rdf:li></rdf:Bag></dc:language><xmp:ModifyDate>2026-08-07T12:37:43+02:00</xmp:ModifyDate><xmp:CreateDate>2026-08-07T12:37:43+02:00</xmp:CreateDate><xmpTPg:NPages>32</xmpTPg:NPages><dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format><xmpMM:InstanceID>9XQzr9f1Du1I0fLGVuYc0w==</xmpMM:InstanceID><xmpMM:DocumentID>8fFLI+xyK2XfaEkahihSNw==</xmpMM:DocumentID><xmpMM:RenditionClass>proof</xmpMM:RenditionClass><pdf:PDFVersion>1.7</pdf:PDFVersion></rdf:Description></rdf:RDF></x:xmpmeta><?xpacket end="r"?>
